{"builder":"Criteria the build must meet (the issue's expect lines, word for word):\n- loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\n- npm test passes\n\nSteps:\n1. Read src/config.js, src/app.js, test/config.test.js and README.md. Currently loadConfig is `return JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));`, so invalid JSON throws a SyntaxError. src/app.js catches any error from loadConfig, prints `bad config: <message>` to stderr and exits 1. The existing test 'bad JSON throws' asserts the SyntaxError.\n2. In src/config.js, change loadConfig(path) so that when JSON.parse throws, the error is caught and loadConfig returns {}. Wrap only the JSON.parse call in the try/catch. Keep the readFileSync call outside it, so an error reading the file (for example a missing file) still propagates as it does now. A valid file must still return its parsed object.\n3. Update test/config.test.js to match the new behaviour. Replace the 'bad JSON throws' test with one asserting that `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` returns a value deep-equal to {} and does not throw. Keep the 'reads a config' test. Optionally add a test that a missing file still throws.\n4. Update the README.md sentence about invalid JSON (`stops it with bad config: <message> and exit 1`) so it describes the new behaviour: invalid JSON in the file now yields an empty config, so the app runs on its defaults. Do not change src/app.js unless something actually needs it; its catch block still handles file-read errors.\n5. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure every test passes.\n6. Note in the pull request description that returning {} silently means a user with a typo gets defaults and no message naming the file or the error position, which the issue body says people need. Flag this as a possible follow-up, for example logging a warning. Do not implement anything beyond the criteria above.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head, from the repository root.\n\nCheck 1: loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead.\nRun:\n```\nd=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > \"$d/bad.json\"; printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > \"$d/good.json\"\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const r = loadConfig(process.argv[1]); console.log(JSON.stringify(r), Object.getPrototypeOf(r) === Object.prototype, Object.keys(r).length);\" \"$d/bad.json\"\n```\nAlso open src/config.js and confirm that loadConfig is defined there and calls JSON.parse inside a try/catch (or an equivalent catch).\nPASS: the command exits 0 without throwing, prints `{} true 0`, and src/config.js's loadConfig catches the error thrown by JSON.parse.\nFAIL: the command throws (for example a SyntaxError stack trace), exits non-zero, or prints anything other than an empty plain object. Also FAIL if the {} result comes from somewhere other than catching the JSON.parse error in loadConfig in src/config.js.\n\nCheck 2: npm test passes.\nRun: `npm test` (with a fresh `npm install` first if package.json declares dependencies).\nPASS: the command exits 0, and the node test runner output shows no failing tests.\nFAIL: a non-zero exit, or any test reported as failing.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS/FAIL):\n- Run the same node command on \"$d/good.json\". It should print `{\"port\":9000} ...`, meaning valid files still parse.\n- Call loadConfig on a path that does not exist. Ideally it still throws, because the expect line covers only the JSON.parse error.\n- Check that the old 'bad JSON throws' test in test/config.test.js was replaced by one asserting {}, rather than deleted with nothing in its place.\n- Check that README.md no longer claims invalid JSON exits with `bad config:` and exit 1.\n- Run `node src/app.js \"$d/bad.json\"` and note what it does. Expect `listening on 8080` with exit 0. Note that this gives the user no message naming the broken file, which the issue body says people need."}